BEGIN_PGML We know the coefficients of the Taylor series for [`g(x)`] centered at [`a=0`] are given by [`\frac{g^{(k)}(0)}{k!}\text{,}`] so we start calculating some derivatives. Note that we can write [`g(x)=(1+x^2)^{-1}\text{.}`] Calculate [`g'(x)\text{,}`] and [`g''(x)\text{.}`]
[`g'(x)={}`][_]{'-2x(1+x^2)^(-2)'}{20} and [`g''(x)={}`][_]{'2(3x^2-1)(1+x^2)^(-3)'}{20}
END_PGML
Section::End();
############################################################ # Section ############################################################ Section::Begin("");
BEGIN_PGML If you simplify [`g''(x)\text{,}`] take the derivative again, and simplify the result, you'd find that
[``` g'''(x) = \frac{24x - 24x^3}{(1+x^2)^4} \text{.}```]
If we don't rewrite [`g'''(x)`] from the form shown above, what derivative rules would be needed to calculate [`g^{(4)}(x)\text{?}`] Select all that apply.
[_]{$mc}{5}
END_PGML
Section::End();
############################################################ # Section ############################################################ Section::Begin("");
BEGIN_PGML Find [`g(0)\text{,}`] [`g'(0)\text{,}`] [`g''(0)\text{,}`] and [`g'''(0)\text{.}`]
[_]{'1'}{5}
[_]{'0'}{5}
[_]{'-2'}{5}
[_]{'0'}{5}
Use the results to determine [`T_3(x)\text{.}`]
[_]{'1-x^2'}{5}
END_PGML
